WEBVTT

00:00:00.181 --> 00:00:02.073
Last time we found where a ball lands

00:00:02.113 --> 00:00:03.321
when it's launched from a cliff.

00:00:04.007 --> 00:00:05.298
Now there's something in the way.

00:00:06.164 --> 00:00:08.530
This ball leaves a cliff top fifteen metres

00:00:08.652 --> 00:00:11.098
above the sea, at eighteen metres per second,

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:13.345
thirty-five degrees above the horizontal.

00:00:14.168 --> 00:00:16.694
Twenty-five metres out stands a wall, eight metres

00:00:16.735 --> 00:00:18.358
tall, measured from sea level.

00:00:19.062 --> 00:00:20.711
The wall is lower than the cliff, so

00:00:21.094 --> 00:00:21.898
surely it clears?

00:00:22.684 --> 00:00:24.292
And what if the ball were launched straight

00:00:24.353 --> 00:00:25.680
sideways at the same speed?

00:00:26.426 --> 00:00:27.011
Make a prediction.

00:00:30.849 --> 00:00:32.315
Here's the thirty-five degree launch.

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:33.684
It sails over.

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:36.457
Now the sideways launch, same speed.

00:00:37.324 --> 00:00:37.952
It hits the wall.

00:00:38.601 --> 00:00:40.888
So a wall lower than the cliff guarantees

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:41.550
nothing.

00:00:42.273 --> 00:00:44.356
What matters is how high the ball is

00:00:44.498 --> 00:00:46.701
at one moment, the moment it reaches the

00:00:46.742 --> 00:00:48.104
wall's horizontal position.

00:00:48.706 --> 00:00:50.611
How far above the wall top it passes

00:00:50.672 --> 00:00:52.436
then is called the clearance.

00:00:53.202 --> 00:00:54.915
Finding it is our whole question.

00:00:55.609 --> 00:00:56.214
Let's set it up.

00:00:56.778 --> 00:00:58.883
Put the origin at the launch point, with

00:00:59.085 --> 00:01:01.932
up as positive, so a y is minus

00:01:02.013 --> 00:01:03.978
nine point eight metres per second squared.

00:01:04.641 --> 00:01:06.949
The sea is fifteen metres below the launch

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:09.078
point, and the wall top is eight metres

00:01:09.199 --> 00:01:09.861
above the sea.

00:01:10.644 --> 00:01:12.624
Eighteen metres per second is the size of

00:01:12.645 --> 00:01:15.886
the launch velocity, not either component, so resolve

00:01:15.927 --> 00:01:16.026
it:

00:01:16.767 --> 00:01:20.648
u x is eighteen cos thirty-five, about fourteen

00:01:20.709 --> 00:01:23.350
point seven metres per second, and u y

00:01:23.531 --> 00:01:26.811
is eighteen sin thirty-five, about ten point three,

00:01:27.153 --> 00:01:27.452
upwards.

00:01:28.153 --> 00:01:30.491
Keep the unrounded values in your calculator.

00:01:31.356 --> 00:01:33.963
And the wall is twenty-five metres away horizontally.

00:01:35.189 --> 00:01:37.319
Which column can tell us when the ball

00:01:37.401 --> 00:01:38.264
reaches the wall?

00:01:39.171 --> 00:01:40.820
Not the vertical one, because the height there

00:01:40.861 --> 00:01:42.068
is exactly what we don't know.

00:01:42.552 --> 00:01:44.476
But we do know the horizontal distance,

00:01:45.178 --> 00:01:48.004
and horizontally the ball keeps a constant velocity.

00:01:48.646 --> 00:01:51.029
So s x equals u x t,

00:01:51.551 --> 00:01:54.475
which gives t equals twenty-five over fourteen point

00:01:54.536 --> 00:01:57.200
seven four, about one point six nine six

00:01:57.241 --> 00:01:57.581
seconds.

00:01:58.302 --> 00:02:00.663
Two columns, one clock, but this time the

00:02:00.744 --> 00:02:03.385
horizontal column sets the clock, and the time

00:02:03.426 --> 00:02:04.026
carries across.

00:02:04.702 --> 00:02:06.998
Now use that time in the vertical column.

00:02:07.682 --> 00:02:09.952
s y equals u y t plus a

00:02:10.034 --> 00:02:11.400
half a y t squared.

00:02:12.124 --> 00:02:13.805
The first term is how high the launch

00:02:13.845 --> 00:02:16.005
velocity alone would carry the ball by then,

00:02:16.386 --> 00:02:18.406
ten point three two times one point six

00:02:18.467 --> 00:02:18.988
nine six,

00:02:19.548 --> 00:02:21.309
about seventeen point five metres.

00:02:22.070 --> 00:02:23.950
The second term is how far gravity has

00:02:23.991 --> 00:02:25.891
pulled it back down in the same time,

00:02:26.473 --> 00:02:29.393
four point nine t squared, about fourteen point

00:02:29.554 --> 00:02:30.034
one metres.

00:02:30.756 --> 00:02:33.003
Subtract, and the ball is three point four

00:02:33.084 --> 00:02:35.292
two metres above the launch point when it

00:02:35.373 --> 00:02:36.095
reaches the wall.

00:02:36.799 --> 00:02:39.270
It's already past its peak and falling, but

00:02:39.291 --> 00:02:40.758
it's still above the cliff top.

00:02:41.318 --> 00:02:41.911
Careful here.

00:02:42.375 --> 00:02:44.679
Three point four two metres is measured from

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:47.264
the launch point, but the wall is measured

00:02:47.305 --> 00:02:47.865
from the sea.

00:02:48.749 --> 00:02:51.098
Heights only compare when they're measured from one

00:02:51.219 --> 00:02:53.628
datum, one level that every height is measured

00:02:53.669 --> 00:02:53.829
from.

00:02:54.413 --> 00:02:55.036
Take sea level.

00:02:55.782 --> 00:02:57.642
The launch point is fifteen metres up,

00:02:58.144 --> 00:03:00.444
so the ball is at fifteen plus three

00:03:00.505 --> 00:03:01.225
point four two,

00:03:01.806 --> 00:03:04.007
eighteen point four two metres above the sea.

00:03:04.728 --> 00:03:06.037
The wall top is at eight.

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:08.823
So the clearance is eighteen point four two

00:03:09.045 --> 00:03:11.850
minus eight, ten point four two, about ten

00:03:11.911 --> 00:03:12.611
point four metres.

00:03:13.317 --> 00:03:14.168
Yes, it clears.

00:03:14.864 --> 00:03:15.997
Here's a trap worth seeing.

00:03:16.602 --> 00:03:17.584
Suppose you round u

00:03:18.106 --> 00:03:20.412
y to ten point three two and the

00:03:20.452 --> 00:03:22.718
time to one point seven zero seconds,

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:25.285
then round each term to three figures.

00:03:26.148 --> 00:03:29.443
Seventeen point five minus fourteen point two gives

00:03:29.624 --> 00:03:30.569
three point three.

00:03:31.232 --> 00:03:34.281
That's twelve centimetres too low, and the clearance

00:03:34.322 --> 00:03:36.528
comes out as ten point three instead of

00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:37.371
ten point four.

00:03:38.456 --> 00:03:42.204
Subtracting two big, nearly equal numbers magnifies any

00:03:42.325 --> 00:03:42.966
rounding in them.

00:03:43.468 --> 00:03:45.634
So keep the full values in your calculator,

00:03:45.996 --> 00:03:47.599
and round only the final answer.

00:03:48.141 --> 00:03:50.189
Now the sideways launch that hit the wall.

00:03:50.814 --> 00:03:51.403
Same steps.

00:03:52.113 --> 00:03:54.785
It reaches the wall after twenty-five over eighteen,

00:03:55.247 --> 00:03:56.513
one point three nine seconds.

00:03:57.175 --> 00:03:59.780
It started with no vertical velocity, so in

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:02.384
that time it simply falls four point nine

00:04:02.625 --> 00:04:05.229
t squared, about nine point four five metres.

00:04:05.851 --> 00:04:07.935
That leaves it about five point five metres

00:04:08.116 --> 00:04:10.079
above the sea, around two and a half

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:11.622
metres below the wall top.

00:04:12.526 --> 00:04:14.152
It gets to the wall sooner, but it

00:04:14.213 --> 00:04:16.744
has no upward velocity to lift it first.

00:04:17.388 --> 00:04:19.634
The launch angle changes both when the ball

00:04:19.675 --> 00:04:21.379
arrives and how high it is.

00:04:22.128 --> 00:04:24.215
So here's the method for any obstacle.

00:04:25.101 --> 00:04:25.627
Where is it?

00:04:26.353 --> 00:04:27.539
When does the ball get there?

00:04:28.285 --> 00:04:30.038
How high is the ball at that time?

00:04:30.744 --> 00:04:32.912
Then compare, on one datum.

00:04:33.597 --> 00:04:36.435
Where, when, how high, compare.

00:04:37.339 --> 00:04:39.546
Notice the clock runs the other way from

00:04:39.567 --> 00:04:40.529
a landing problem.

00:04:41.092 --> 00:04:43.319
There, the vertical motion gave us the time.

00:04:43.842 --> 00:04:46.743
Here, the obstacle's horizontal position gives the time,

00:04:47.185 --> 00:04:49.525
and the vertical column turns that time into

00:04:49.566 --> 00:04:49.866
a height.

00:04:53.658 --> 00:04:54.127
Your turn.

00:04:55.075 --> 00:04:57.396
A ball is launched at twenty point zero

00:04:57.457 --> 00:05:00.539
metres per second, thirty degrees above the horizontal,

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:03.321
from a platform two point zero metres above

00:05:03.382 --> 00:05:03.961
level ground.

00:05:04.703 --> 00:05:07.112
A wall three point zero metres high stands

00:05:07.314 --> 00:05:09.864
twenty point zero metres away horizontally.

00:05:10.507 --> 00:05:13.696
Neglecting air resistance, determine whether the ball clears

00:05:13.737 --> 00:05:15.300
the wall, and by how much.

00:05:16.085 --> 00:05:16.989
Pause here to answer.

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:31.042
u x is twenty cos thirty, about seventeen

00:05:31.082 --> 00:05:31.582
point three,

00:05:32.123 --> 00:05:34.304
so the ball reaches the wall after twenty

00:05:34.484 --> 00:05:36.925
over seventeen point three, one point one five

00:05:36.965 --> 00:05:37.286
seconds.

00:05:38.106 --> 00:05:40.552
Then s y is ten t minus four

00:05:40.592 --> 00:05:43.338
point nine t squared, about plus five point

00:05:43.358 --> 00:05:44.360
zero one metres.

00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:47.442
On the ground datum that's two plus five

00:05:47.482 --> 00:05:49.963
point zero one, about seven point zero one

00:05:50.003 --> 00:05:50.343
metres,

00:05:50.904 --> 00:05:52.625
so it clears the three metre wall by

00:05:52.645 --> 00:05:54.366
about four point zero one metres.

00:05:55.208 --> 00:05:57.573
The classic slip is comparing five point zero

00:05:57.694 --> 00:05:59.819
one with three and forgetting the platform.

00:06:01.469 --> 00:06:01.934
Last one.

00:06:02.540 --> 00:06:04.370
A ball is launched from a point twelve

00:06:04.432 --> 00:06:06.081
point zero metres above the ground.

00:06:06.883 --> 00:06:08.604
When it reaches a wall, it is two

00:06:08.685 --> 00:06:11.127
point six metres below its launch point.

00:06:11.850 --> 00:06:14.100
The wall is nine point zero metres tall.

00:06:14.724 --> 00:06:16.958
Determine whether the ball clears the wall, and

00:06:16.979 --> 00:06:17.562
by how much.

00:06:18.290 --> 00:06:19.120
Pause here to answer.

00:06:29.869 --> 00:06:31.944
Put both heights on the ground datum.

00:06:32.669 --> 00:06:34.634
The ball is at twelve minus two point

00:06:34.675 --> 00:06:34.914
six,

00:06:35.417 --> 00:06:37.361
nine point four metres above the ground.

00:06:38.023 --> 00:06:39.766
The wall top is at nine point zero,

00:06:40.088 --> 00:06:42.252
so the ball clears it by zero point

00:06:42.334 --> 00:06:42.914
four metres,

00:06:43.416 --> 00:06:44.558
just forty centimetres.

00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:47.700
The slip is seeing minus two point six

00:06:47.821 --> 00:06:49.101
and deciding it must hit,

00:06:49.603 --> 00:06:52.164
or comparing two point six with nine directly.

00:06:52.887 --> 00:06:54.703
Heights only compare on the same datum.

00:06:56.328 --> 00:06:58.298
So to clear a wall, find when the

00:06:58.339 --> 00:07:00.268
ball gets there, then how high it is.

00:07:01.152 --> 00:07:03.073
Now take the wall away and put a

00:07:03.134 --> 00:07:04.335
target on level ground,

00:07:04.976 --> 00:07:07.518
seventy-two metres from a launcher firing at thirty

00:07:07.579 --> 00:07:08.379
metres per second.

00:07:09.062 --> 00:07:10.127
What angle should it aim at?

00:07:10.973 --> 00:07:13.100
It turns out there are two answers, and

00:07:13.161 --> 00:07:14.084
next time we'll see why.

00:07:16.961 --> 00:07:17.784
Here are the key notes.

00:07:18.307 --> 00:07:19.855
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:19.895 --> 00:07:20.317
to copy them.
